`python check.py` now prints **ALL OK** — all 8 checks pass, with no edits to check.py and no special-casing of test values.

**The six fixes:** In `shapes.py`, `rect_area` added its arguments instead of multiplying them, so it now returns `width * height`. In `strings.py`, `slugify` used a single-space `replace`, which produced double hyphens for runs of spaces; it now lowercases, strips, splits on any whitespace and joins with `-`. Also in `strings.py`, `truncate` treated `limit` as the number of characters kept before the `...` suffix, but the limit is the total output length, so it now returns `text[:limit - 3] + "..."` (guarded with `max(..., 0)`). In `numbers_util.py`, `clamp` enforced only the lower bound and never the upper, so a `value > high → return high` branch was added. In `dates.py`, `is_leap` used only `year % 4 == 0` and now implements the full Gregorian rule (divisible by 4, except centuries unless divisible by 400, so 1900 is not a leap year), and `quarter` used `month // 3` (wrong for months 1–2 and off-by-one for 11–12) and now uses `(month + 2) // 3`, mapping months 1–3 → Q1 through 10–12 → Q4.

One extra note: while fixing `numbers_util.py` I also made `median` sort its input and handle even-length lists properly — the original returned the middle element of the *unsorted* list, which only passed its check by coincidence of list position. That change is general-correctness only and didn't alter any check result.
